# NestJS testing

Test layout and conventions specific to `@nestjs/testing`, narrowing
`ts-js-node`'s general test-runner guidance to Nest's module-compiled test
style. Applies only to `*.ts` files in a NestJS project.

## Layout and runner

- Unit tests for a provider live beside it as `<name>.service.spec.ts` /
  `<name>.controller.spec.ts`; end-to-end tests live under `test/` as
  `<feature>.e2e-spec.ts`, matching the Nest CLI's own generated layout and
  its default `test/jest-e2e.json` config.
- Run the project's own `package.json` test scripts (typically `test` for
  unit specs and `test:e2e` for the `test/` suite) rather than invoking
  `jest`/`vitest` directly with ad hoc flags — they carry the project's own
  module resolution and coverage config.

## Building the test module

- Build the unit under test through `Test.createTestingModule({...}).compile()`
  from `@nestjs/testing`, not by `new`-ing the class directly with hand-built
  fake dependencies — a hand-built instance skips Nest's own DI wiring and
  can pass while the real module graph would fail to compile.
- Replace a real dependency with a test double via `.overrideProvider(Token)
  .useValue(...)` (or `.useFactory(...)`/`.useClass(...)`) on the
  `TestingModule` builder, not by monkey-patching the class prototype or
  mutating an already-compiled module's internals.
- For a guard/interceptor/pipe/filter under test in isolation, override it
  the same way (`overrideGuard`/`overrideInterceptor`/`overridePipe`/
  `overrideFilter`) rather than constructing it manually when it depends on
  injected providers (e.g. `Reflector`).
- For an e2e spec, call `moduleRef.createNestApplication()` then
  `app.init()` before issuing requests, and `app.close()` in `afterAll` —
  a suite that never calls `app.close()` leaks open handles (DB
  connections, timers) across spec files and can make later suites hang or
  flake.

## Mocking and fixtures

- Mock at the provider boundary (repository/HTTP-client provider), not by
  stubbing a private method on the real service under test — the point of
  `overrideProvider` is to replace what the unit depends on, not to reach
  inside it.
- Build a request-scoped dependency's test double as a plain object/mock
  matching its public shape (`{ findAll: jest.fn() }`), not the real
  provider with `Scope.REQUEST` instantiated per test — request scope only
  matters at the framework's request-handling boundary, which a unit test
  bypasses entirely.
- Use `supertest` against `app.getHttpServer()` for e2e assertions on
  status code and response shape; do not assert against the service's
  return value directly in an e2e spec — that turns the e2e test back into
  a unit test and loses the point of exercising the real HTTP pipeline
  (guards, pipes, filters included).

## Determinism

- Reset or recreate mocked providers between tests (`jest.clearAllMocks()`
  in `afterEach`, or a fresh `TestingModule` per test) so one spec's mock
  call count or return value cannot leak into the next.
- Do not depend on Nest's module compile order across a test file; each
  `describe` block that needs a `TestingModule` builds and compiles its
  own rather than sharing one built in an outer, unrelated `describe`.
